@defgena{documentation,@lowpackage{}} client package

Return the documentation of @var{package} @emph{in the package system
specified by @var{client}}.

The returned documentation is either a string or @code{nil}.

The function can be used to implement the behavior of calls
@code{(@speclink{f,documentation} @var{package} t)}
where @var{package} is a package object with the following properties

@enumerate
@item

@code{(@genref{packagep,@lowpackage{}} @var{client} @var{package}) @result{} @emph{true}}

@item

@code{(typep @var{package} '@lowpackage{}:package) @result{} @emph{true}}
@end enumerate
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf documentation),@lowpackage{}} new-value client package

Set the document of @var{package} to @var{new-value} @emph{in the
package system specified by @var{client}}.

@var{package} is a package object. @var{new-value} is a string or @code{nil}.

The function can be used to implement the behavior of calls
@code{(setf (@speclink{f,documentation} @var{package} t) @var{new-value})}
where @var{package} is a package object with the following properties

@enumerate
@item

@code{(@genref{packagep,@lowpackage{}} @var{client} @var{package})
@result{} @emph{true}}

@item

@code{(typep @var{package} '@lowpackage{}:package) @result{} @emph{true}}
@end enumerate
@end deffn

@defgena{make-package-object,@lowpackage{}} client name

Create and return a new package object named @var{name} that is suitable
for the package system specified by @var{client}.

@var{name} is a string.

In contrast to the behavior of @speclink{f,make-package}, the returned
package contains no nicknames, no relations to other packages, no
symbols and it is not registered under the name @var{name} in the
package system.  Those aspects have to be set up by separate calls to
other low-level functions.  Furthermore, whether @var{name} already
names a different package is not considered.
@end deffn

@node Low Package-symbol Relation Functions
@section Low Package-symbol Relation Functions

@cindex symbol entry
@cindex export status
@cindex shadow status
This protocol uses the concept of a @term{symbol entry} which is a
triple consisting of a @speclink{t,symbol} object, an
@term{export status} and a @term{shadow status}.  The information in
such a triple is associated with a symbol name within a package to
indicate the status of the symbol in that package in terms of the symbol
being present in the package, the symbol being external and the symbol
being shadowing.  Concretely, the @termref{export status} is either
@code{:internal} or @code{:external} while the @termref{shadow status} is
either @code{t} or @code{nil}.

;;; TODO: old The SYMBOL ENTRIES of a package is a list of CONS cells, each
;;; representing a present symbol and its status.  The CAR of such a
;;; CONS cell is the symbol itself, and the CDR is the status which is
;;; one of :INTERNAL, :INTERNAL-SHADOWING, :EXTERNAL, and
;;; :EXTERNAL-SHADOWING.

@defgena{map-symbol-entries,@lowpackage{}} client function package @
  @optional{} status

Call @var{function} for each @termref{symbol entry} in @var{package}
@emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}} and return
@code{nil}.

@var{function} is a function the lambda list of which has to be
compatible with @code{(symbol export-status shadow-status)} where
@var{symbol} is the symbol object for which the @termref{symbol entry}
is reported, @var{export-status} indicates the @termref{export status}
of @var{symbol} and @var{shadow-status} indicates the @termref{shadow
status} of @var{symbol}.  Any values returned by @var{function} are
ignored.

As is probably obvious, this function does not consider inherited
symbols in any way.
@end deffn

@defgena{symbol-entries,@lowpackage{}} client package &optional status

@quotation warning
Do we keep this function or remove it?
@end quotation

Return as a list of triples the @termrefl{symbol entry, symbol entries}
of @var{package} @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}.

The elements of the returned list of the form @code{(symbol
export-status shadow-status)}.
@end deffn

@defgena{symbol-entry,@lowpackage{}} client name package

Look up the @termref{symbol entry} for @var{name} in @var{package}
@emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}} and return three
values:

@enumerate

@item
The symbol object if such an entry exists in @var{package} or @code{nil}
otherwise.

@item
@cindex export status
The @termref{export status} of the symbol in @var{package} if an entry
exists or @code{nil} otherwise.  The export status is either
@code{:internal} or @code{:external}.

@item
@cindex shadow status
A Boolean which indicates the @termref{shadow status} of the symbol in
@var{package}.  @code{t} indicates that the symbol named @var{name} is
shadowing in @var{package}.  @code{nil} indicates that the symbol is not
shadowing, either because it is not interned in package or because it is
interned but not shadowing.

@end enumerate

The most general way to check whether @var{name} names a symbol that is
interned in @var{package} is to check whether the second return value is
@code{nil}.  This is because the first return value can be @code{nil} if
the package system in question uses ordinary @commonlisp{} symbols as
its symbol objects and because the third return value can be @code{nil}
whether the entry exists or not.

As is probably obvious, this function does not consider inherited
symbols in any way.
@end deffn

@defgena{set-symbol-entry,@lowpackage{}} symbol export-status shadow-status @
  client name package

@cindex symbol entry
Create or change the @termref{symbol entry} for @var{name} in
@var{package} @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}.

@cindex export status
@cindex shadow status
@var{symbol} is the symbol of the new or changed symbol entry.
@var{export-status} is the @termref{export status} of the new or changed
symbol entry.  @var{export-status} has to be either @code{:internal} or
@code{:external}.  @var{shadow-status} is the @termref{shadow status} of
the new or changed symbol entry.  @var{shadow-status} has to be either
@code{nil} or @code{t}.

This function does not adjust the home package of @var{symbol} nor
consider a potentially existing symbol entry for @var{name}.  This
function also does not check for or handle any conflicts that are caused
by the changes of the export status and shadow status of @var{name} in
@var{package}.

@quotation Note
There is a @code{setf} expander for @code{symbol-entry} which expands to
@code{set-symbol-entry} so that clients can use the syntax
@code{(setf (symbol-entry @var{client} @var{name} @var{package})
            (values @var{symbol} @var{export-status} @var{shadow-status}))}
to call @code{set-symbol-entry}.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@node Low Environment Functions
@section Low Environment Functions

@defgena{packages,@lowpackage{}} client

Return a list of all defined packages @emph{in the package system
specified by @var{client}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{find-package,@lowpackage{}} client name

Return the package object that has @var{name} as its name or one of its
nicknames @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}.
Return @code{nil} if no package has @var{name} as its name or as one of
its nicknames.
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf find-package),@lowpackage{}} new-value client name

Set the package associated with @var{name} to @var{new-value} @emph{in
the package system specified by @var{client}}.  @var{new-value} is
either @code{nil} or a package object.  If @var{new-value} is
@code{nil}, arrange for @var{name} to be associated with no package.

In accordance with the role of the low module, this function does not
check or adjust the name and nicknames of @var{new-value} if
@var{new-value} is a package object.  Neither does this function check
whether @var{name} is associated with a package at the time of the call.
@end deffn
